UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 9, Lesson 2: Multiplying by 10 and 100', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Multiplying by 10 or 100 shifts digits one or two places left; zeros hold empty places so the value scales correctly.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nCalculate 70 × 10 and 70 × 100. Explain the place shift.\n\nFEEDBACK\n700; 7,000 — digits shift one/two places; zeros hold places.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSaying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later; keep the shift idea.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSolve twenty ×10/×100 problems including multiples of 10. Explain the place shift for eight answers.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"Multiplying by 10 or 100 shifts digits one or two places left; zeros hold empty places so the value scales correctly.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Strategies","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-MR-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Solve twenty ×10/×100 problems including multiples of 10. Explain the place shift for eight answers. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for ×10; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Saying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later;. Correction uses 700; 7,000 — digits shift one/two places; zeros hold places.. Why: restores accurate ×10.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Saying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later; keep the shift idea. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Solve one fresh problem that uses ×10 with different numbers from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Describe one likely mistake with ×10, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept any mathematically valid solution that uses new numbers, shows working for ×10, and names places/values. Reject copied model numbers. / Accept a named misconception about ×10, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"700; 7,000 — digits shift one/two places; zeros hold places is correct because This matches the taught method for ×10. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","700; 7,000 — digits shift one/two places; zeros hold places","Saying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later"],"question":"Calculate 70 × 10 and 70 × 100. Explain the place shift. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for ×10; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of ×100 and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Saying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later","Multiplying by 10 or 100 shifts digits one or two places left","700; 7,000 — digits shift one/two places; zeros hold places"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with ×10?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named ×100 and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply ×10 to new material and name the ×100 and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Saying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later","Name ×100 and explain why it matters only, without linking it to ×10"],"question":"Which response best applies ×10 with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Mathematics answers need labelled working tied to place value, quantity or the asked operation. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change digits to look neater without checking place value","Saying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later","Show each place, value or step and say why that step is valid","Write only a final number with no working"],"question":"Which working best supports a correct answer for this lesson on ×10?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the taught ×10 method; the others break place value, units or the asked quantity. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Saying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later","Read 70 from left to right as ones only, ignoring place value","Ignore units and treat 70 and 10 as interchangeable","700; 7,000 — digits shift one/two places; zeros hold places"],"question":"Using the method for ×10, which statement is true?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,