s from the worked model. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Sample: new numbers solved with visible working for ×10; key places/values named. Do not award marks for retyping the model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a named misconception about ×10, corrected working, and a method reason. Reject vague “I was wrong” with no repair.","question":"Describe one likely mistake with ×10, show corrected working, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Saying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later;. Correction uses 700; 7,000 — digits shift one/two places; zeros hold places.. Why: restores accurate ×10.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y4-w09-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise ×10.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Number and Algebra","success_criteria":["I can use ×10 with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify ×100 and explain why it matters.","I can practise place value independently on a new prompt.","I can notice zero needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Multiplying by 10 or 100 shifts digits one or two places left; zeros hold empty places so the value scales correctly.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Multiplying by 10 or 100 shifts digits one or two places left","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about ×10 with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n34 × 10 = 340; 34 × 100 = 3,400. Digits move; zeros fill ones/tens as needed. Not “add a zero” as a magic rule without place meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nCalculate 70 × 10 and 70 × 100. Explain the place shift.\n\nFEEDBACK\n700; 7,000 — digits shift one/two places; zeros hold places.","explain":"Multiplying by 10 or 100 shifts digits one or two places left; zeros hold empty places so the value scales correctly.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later; keep the shift idea. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Place-value shift for ×10 and ×100.","caption":"Zeros hold places.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">×10</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">34 → 340</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">×100</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">34 → 3,400</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: 34 × 10 = 340; 34 × 100 = 3,400. Digits move; zeros fill ones/tens as needed. Not “add a zero” as a magic rule without p Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: 34 × 10 = 340; 34 × 100 = 3,400. Digits move; zeros fill ones/tens as needed. Not “add a zero” as a magic rule without p Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about ×10 with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n34 × 10 = 340; 34 × 100 = 3,400. Digits move; zeros fill ones/tens as needed. Not “add a zero” as a magic rule without place meaning. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Show the working","visual":[{"alt":"Place shift contrasted with empty magic rule.","caption":"Explain eight shifts.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Shift</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">digits left</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Myth</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">add zero magic only</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about ×10 with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nSaying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later; keep the shift idea. Here is a more productive model: 34 × 10 = 340; 34 × 100 = 3,400. Digits move; zeros fill ones/tens as needed. Not “add a zero” as a magic rule without place meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then write corrected working.\n\nFEEDBACK\n700; 7,000 — digits shift one/two places; zeros hold places.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Saying “add zeros” without place value can fail for decimals later; keep the shift idea. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example","A guess with no working","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about ×10 with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMultiplying by 10 or 100 shifts digits one or two places left; zeros hold empty places so the value scales correctly. Now choose different numbers to show you can transfer the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your answer.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","expla